Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Offset, Sharp (intersection join): a crease that turns from concave to
convex inside its edge, a crease tangent at both its ends, and a face
vanishing to a point where a further face touches it (mitcad#2418, GitHub
mitcad/Mitcad#641).

The case: a loft from a circle of radius 170 at z = 315 to one of 85 at
z = 0 centred at x = 225, a cylinder on each end, mirrored about x = 0 and
joined, the crotch rounded 20; shelled 5 mm inside with the three ends open.
The rims where the loft meets the cylinders are concave on one side and
convex on the other, the faces tangent at 67.8 degrees round them. The
thick solid was not done at any thickness ("the offset faces do not close
up" in MitCad); without the rounding the same.

1. BRepOffset_Tool::Inter3D, JoinCreaseAcrossInnerTangency (new, before
   DropCrossingsPastTangentEnd): the enlarged offsets of a crease's faces
   are tangent at the offset of the point where the crease turns, and
   there their intersection is an X: the branch along the crease on both
   sides and a branch crossing it, joined by pieces of a few microns, the
   branch along the crease not always connected across (5 micrometres
   apart), or running on from the crossing branch into it. CheckIntersFF
   and the removal of excess edges kept the crossing branch alone, or the
   crease's piece on one side. The turns are found on the pair's crease
   edges (the faces' normals' cross product along the edge changes sign;
   where they fold back, nothing); a piece passing a tangency inside it is
   split there; at each tangency, within a neighbourhood of a tenth of a
   millimetre (a tenth of the offset at most), pieces of the knot and
   pieces leaving across the crease (their chord over half a millimetre)
   are dropped, the two along it share one vertex there, and the pieces
   connected to them are the intersection. Where the crease is one edge
   tangent at both ends (two lofts' sides meeting along a curve that fades
   out at both ends), the offsets of its ends are taken alike, and only the
   branch into the edge is kept there (before, a piece of 2 micrometres).
   Rebuilt pieces get a curve of their own (a trimmed copy, the same
   parameter) and their vertices' parameters on them: a vertex on two
   pieces of one closed curve of intersection got the parameter of the one
   it was put on last, and TrimEdge ran a piece back over the crossing
   branch. Past a turn the crease is of the other kind than at the edge's
   middle (whose kind the analysis gives the edge): those pieces are
   oriented in the offset faces the other way. Where no turn is found, or
   the pieces at it are not one branch along the crease on each side and
   crossing ones, nothing changes.
2. BRepOffset_Inter3d::ConnexIntByInt, the distribution of a face pair's
   pieces among its edges (patch 2140): an edge keeps too the pieces
   nearest to its own points, as a copy of its own where another edge is
   the one nearest the piece's middle (the convex part of the crease runs
   round the far side of the loft's periodic face from one edge's end to
   the other's: one piece, trimmed once at both ends' neighbours, kept the
   run between them).
3. BRepOffset_Inter3d::ConnexIntByInt: a face vanishing to a point at a
   vertex (a degenerated edge there), its other edges at the vertex
   tangential, where a further face meets the vertex it shares no edge
   with (the rounding's tip at the top circle, the cylinder): the tip of
   its offset lies 0.64 mm past the further face's offset. Their offsets
   are intersected, and the piece nearest the tip is the image of the
   degenerated edge, stored for both faces, oriented in the further face
   to run on from its edge's image at the vertex.
   BRepOffset_Inter2d::ConnexIntByInt: in the further face's offset, the
   images of the two edges meeting at that vertex meet that image each,
   not each other.
4. TrimEdges (BRepOffset_MakeOffset.cxx), InsideChainOfPieces: a piece of an
   edge's image joined at both ends to other pieces of it, and through them
   to a piece its neighbours meet, is not removed as one without
   intersections: its joints are its ends (the convex part between the
   joints at the turns).
With MITCAD_TRACE_OFFSET_CREASE set, JoinCreaseAcrossInnerTangency says on
the standard error the tangencies, the pieces at them and what it keeps.

The patch's test and its before/after proof:
third_party/vcpkg-ports/docs/opencascade-patch-tests.md, section 2418.

